✅ MySQL:
Developer: Originally MySQL AB, now owned by Oracle Corporation.

License: Open-source + proprietary (dual licensing).

Updates: Slower, controlled by Oracle.

Community: Limited community involvement due to corporate ownership.

Performance: Solid and reliable, especially for enterprise use.

Features: May lag behind in newer features compared to MariaDB.

Compatibility: Industry standard; widely used and supported.

✅ MariaDB:
Developer: Fork of MySQL by the original creators.

License: Fully open-source (GPL).

Updates: Faster release cycles, frequent feature updates.

Community: Strong community-driven development.

Performance: Often faster due to optimizations and new engines.

Features: Includes additional storage engines and advanced features not in MySQL.

Compatibility: Drop-in replacement for MySQL (same commands, clients, libraries).

⚖️ Summary Table:
| Feature              | MySQL                    | MariaDB                        |
| -------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ |
| **Owner**            | Oracle Corporation       | MariaDB Foundation             |
| **License**          | Open-source + Commercial | Fully open-source (GPL)        |
| **Speed of Updates** | Slower                   | Faster and more frequent       |
| **New Features**     | Conservative             | More aggressive & innovative   |
| **Storage Engines**  | Fewer                    | More (Aria, ColumnStore, etc.) |
| **Compatibility**    | Industry standard        | MySQL-compatible (mostly)      |


💡 Note: If you're building enterprise apps and require Oracle support — MySQL might be preferred.
If you want full open-source control with faster innovation — MariaDB is a strong choice.